# NOTE: This is, as of 2026, the latest quantitative FAO foresight exercise on land use.
# The 2022 edition of "The future of food and agriculture" (Drivers and triggers for transformation) is qualitative and does not update these projections.
meta:
  origin:
    producer: Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations
    title: The future of food and agriculture - Alternative pathways to 2050
    description: |-
      FAO's Global Perspectives Studies report "The future of food and agriculture - Alternative pathways to 2050" explores three scenarios of global food and agriculture up to 2050, based on simulations with the FAO Global Agriculture Perspectives System (GAPS) model:
      - Business As Usual (BAU): a continuation of historical trends.
      - Towards Sustainability (TSS): a shift towards more sustainable food systems.
      - Stratified Societies (SSS): a future of increased inequality.

      Projections are provided for a 2012 base year and the years 2030, 2035, 2040 and 2050.
    title_snapshot: The future of food and agriculture - Alternative pathways to 2050 - Crop production
    description_snapshot: |-
      Country-level projections of crop production indicators (arable land, harvested area, crop yields, and cropping intensity), disaggregated by crop and by production system (rainfed or irrigated), for the three scenarios of the report.

      Following FAOSTAT terminology, "arable land" is used in this dataset for the physical area under temporary and permanent agricultural crops (which FAOSTAT calls "cropland").
    citation_full: |-
      FAO. 2018. The future of food and agriculture - Alternative pathways to 2050. Rome. 224 pp. Licence: CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO.
    attribution: FAO (2018)
    attribution_short: FAO
    url_main: https://www.fao.org/global-perspectives-studies/food-agriculture-projections-to-2050/en/
    url_download: https://www.fao.org/fileadmin/user_upload/global-perspective/csv/FOFA2050CountryData_Crop-production.csv.zip
    date_accessed: '2026-07-03'
    date_published: '2018'
    license:
      name: CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO
      url: https://openknowledge.fao.org/handle/20.500.14283/I8429EN
